<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tatiana Proskouriakoff</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tatiana Proskouriakoff was a Russian-born American archaeologist and architectural artist who transformed the study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maya-script">Maya writing</a>. In a paper of 1960 she showed that the dated monuments of Piedras Negras recorded the births, accessions and deaths of rulers, proving that Maya inscriptions contained history rather than only calendar and religion. Her work opened the way to the decipherment of Maya hieroglyphs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tatiana-proskouriakoff.jpg" alt="Tatiana Proskouriakoff" width="210" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms. Char Solomon, biographer of Ms. T Proskouriakoff, and owner of site the image is sourced from · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charsolomon_TProskouriakoff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tatiana Avenirovna Proskouriakoff was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a>, in the Tomsk Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, the daughter of a chemist and a physician. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the family went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> had asked her father to oversee the production of munitions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> kept them there. They lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> and then in Lansdowne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, where she graduated as valedictorian of her class. She took American citizenship in 1924.</p><p>In 1926 she enrolled at the Pennsylvania State College School of Architecture, graduating in 1930 as the only woman in her class. As a graduate student at the University of Pennsylvania she drew archaeological illustrations as a volunteer at the University Museum, and through its assistant curator Linton Satterthwaite she joined its excavations at Piedras Negras, in the Usumacinta region, in 1936–37, first to draw the ruined buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her reconstruction drawing of the Piedras Negras acropolis caught the attention of Sylvanus Morley. Unable to persuade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnegie-institution-for-science">Carnegie Institution of Washington</a> to hire her, he raised funds for her to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a> and Yucatán in 1939, and in the early 1940s she became a research associate there. Her analysis of 1942 established that the ruins of Takalik Abaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> were in part Maya. From 1950 to 1955 she worked at the excavations of Mayapan.</p><p>While tracing how the style of Maya sculpture changed over time, she found that the dates on the stelae marked events in the lives of rulers, and from their pattern she showed a sequence of seven rulers over two hundred years. In 1958 she moved to the Peabody Museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>, as honorary curator of Maya art, and worked there until she retired in 1977. In 1967 she wrote the preface to the English translation of a monograph by Yuri Knorozov on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maya-script">Maya writing</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She received the Alfred V. Kidder Medal in 1962 and was elected to the American Philosophical Society in 1981. In her last years she had Alzheimer's disease, and she died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Massachusetts, on 30 August 1985. Her history of the Maya, on which she had worked for over twenty years, was published after her death. On Easter Sunday 1998 her colleague David Stuart carried her ashes to Piedras Negras, where they were interred at the summit of the Acropolis she had once drawn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tatiana Proskouriakoff” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Largely deciphered in the second half of the 20th century, following the work of Yuri Knorozov and <b>Tatiana Proskouriakoff</b>, it now allows scholars to read the inscriptions in which Maya kings recorded their rituals, their accessions and their dealings with the gods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…and its decipherment was one of the great intellectual achievements of the 20th century: Yuri Knorozov argued in 1952 that many of its signs stood for syllables, Heinrich Berlin identified the signs naming kingdoms, and <b>Tatiana Proskouriakoff</b> showed in 1960 that the monuments recorded the lives of rulers.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Analysing these sequences, <b>Proskouriakoff</b> demonstrated that the glyphs named rulers and dated events in their lives.</blockquote><a href="/state/piedras-negras">Piedras Negras</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Watertown, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.49,84.95&amp;year=1958" aria-label="Tomsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6389 243 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tatiana Proskouriakoff"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6839 513h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6839 513h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.49,84.95&amp;year=1958">What was here in 1958? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
